# PIDGN-T Data Exploration Notebook

## Explainable Temporal Graph Learning for Multimodal Neurogenetic Data Integration in Early Parkinson's Disease Prediction


This notebook provides exploratory analysis of the multimodal dataset used in the PIDGN-T framework.

The objective of this notebook is to analyze the characteristics of the input modalities before model training, including:

- Genomic single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) profiles
- Structural MRI-derived features
- Longitudinal clinical and behavioral observations


The analysis focuses on:

1. Dataset statistics
2. Class distribution between Parkinson's disease (PD) and healthy control subjects
3. Missing modality investigation
4. Feature dimensionality analysis
5. Basic quality control procedures


This notebook supports the reproducibility of the preprocessing and experimental pipeline described in:

Section 4.1:
Dataset Description and Preprocessing


Repository:
PIDGN-T
## Dataset Organization

The PIDGN-T framework integrates three heterogeneous biomedical modalities:

### 1. Genomic Modality

Input:
- SNP profiles
- Genetic risk-related features

Representation:
- Genomic Autoencoder embedding


### 2. Neuroimaging Modality

Input:
- Structural MRI volumes

Representation:
- 3D convolutional feature extraction


### 3. Clinical/Behavioral Modality

Input:
- Longitudinal clinical assessments
- Behavioral measurements

Representation:
- Temporal sequence modeling


The final representation is constructed through multimodal graph learning.

# ============================================================
# Import Required Libraries
# ============================================================


import os

import numpy as np

import pandas as pd


import matplotlib.pyplot as plt


import seaborn as sns


from pathlib import Path

## Define Data Paths

The repository assumes the following data organization:

data/

├── splits/

│   ├── train_split.csv

│   ├── validation_split.csv

│   └── test_split.csv


The split files contain subject-level identifiers and corresponding labels.

# ============================================================
# Dataset Paths
# ============================================================


DATA_PATH = Path(
    "../data/splits/"
)


train_file = DATA_PATH / "train_split.csv"

validation_file = DATA_PATH / "validation_split.csv"

test_file = DATA_PATH / "test_split.csv"

# ============================================================
# Load Dataset Splits
# ============================================================


train_data = pd.read_csv(
    train_file
)


validation_data = pd.read_csv(
    validation_file
)


test_data = pd.read_csv(
    test_file
)



print(
    "Training samples:",
    len(train_data)
)


print(
    "Validation samples:",
    len(validation_data)
)


print(
    "Testing samples:",
    len(test_data)
)

## Dataset Preview

The following section displays the first samples of the training dataset.

Each sample corresponds to one participant and contains:

- Subject identifier
- Multimodal features
- Clinical label

train_data.head()


# Class Distribution Analysis


PIDGN-T performs binary Parkinson's disease prediction.

Classes:

- 0 : Healthy Control
- 1 : Parkinson's Disease


The class distribution is analyzed because imbalance between classes may affect model optimization.

Weighted cross-entropy loss is therefore adopted during training.

# ============================================================
# Class Distribution
# ============================================================


label_counts = train_data["label"].value_counts()


plt.figure(
    figsize=(6,4)
)


sns.barplot(
    x=label_counts.index,
    y=label_counts.values
)


plt.xlabel(
    "Class Label"
)


plt.ylabel(
    "Number of Subjects"
)


plt.title(
    "PD and Control Subject Distribution"
)


plt.show()

# Missing Data Analysis


Multimodal biomedical datasets may contain incomplete observations.

This analysis evaluates:

- Missing clinical records
- Missing modality availability
- Data completeness


The generated statistics support the quality-control procedure described in the preprocessing pipeline.

# ============================================================
# Missing Value Analysis
# ============================================================


missing_ratio = (

    train_data.isnull()
    .mean()
    .sort_values(
        ascending=False
    )

)


missing_ratio.head(20)

plt.figure(
    figsize=(10,5)
)


missing_ratio.head(20).plot(
    kind="bar"
)


plt.ylabel(
    "Missing Ratio"
)


plt.title(
    "Top Missing Features"
)


plt.xticks(
    rotation=90
)


plt.show()

# Feature Dimensionality Analysis


The multimodal input space contains heterogeneous feature dimensions:

- High-dimensional genomic SNP features
- MRI-derived representations
- Clinical temporal variables


Understanding dimensionality is important for selecting suitable encoder architectures.

# ============================================================
# Feature Dimension Information
# ============================================================


print(
    "Number of Features:",
    train_data.shape[1]
)


print(
    "Number of Samples:",
    train_data.shape[0]
)

